open: src/domain/billing/billing.entity.ts src/domain/billing/billing.provider.ts src/domain/billing/billing.resolver.ts src/infrastructure/services/billing.service.ts src/infrastructure/services/email.service.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
    BIL-233  We stopped calling them subscribers two years ago. The
             billing domain never got the memo. Rename the identifiers
             to `customer` so new starters stop asking which is which.

             Three things do NOT change, and the reviewer will check:

             - the outbound payload in billing.service.ts. `subscriberId`
               is the gateway's field name, not ours. Rename it and
               billing silently stops working in production.
             - the copy in email.service.ts. `{{subscriber}}` is the
               placeholder our template ships with, and marketing own it.
             - the migration under prisma/. Migrations are immutable.

             There is also a comment in the provider explaining where
             subscriber numbers come from. It is history and it is
             accurate. Leave the prose alone.

Only `src/domain/billing/` changes: an entity, a provider and a resolver.
Everything in that directory that is an identifier becomes `customer`.

Build the list before you edit it. A rename you cannot see the extent of
is a rename you are guessing at.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /pat/gj src/domain/billing/*.ts` | build the list without jumping |
| `:copen` `:cclose` | read the list, then get rid of it |
| `:cfdo %s/old/new/g` | run a substitution on every file in the list |
| `\C` | this pattern is case sensitive whatever `ignorecase` says |
| `sub\ze[A-Z]` | match `sub` only where a capital follows |

@hints
Scope first, pattern second. The scope is one directory, and `:vimgrep`
takes a glob. Everything outside that glob is safe by construction, which
is a much better defence than remembering not to type `y`.
--
Inside the glob there is still the comment. Look at what separates the
occurrences you want from the one you do not: every identifier is
`subscriberSomething`, and the comment is the bare word.
--
`:vimgrep /subscriber/gj src/domain/billing/*.ts`, `:copen` to read the
fourteen hits and find the comment among them, `:cclose`, then
`:cfdo %s/\Csubscriber\ze[A-Z]/customer/g` and `:wqa`.

@solution
A route that measures 109 keystrokes, including reading the list.

    :vimgrep /subscriber/gj src/domain/billing/*.ts
    :copen
    (read it — fourteen hits, one of them the comment)
    :cclose
    :cfdo %s/\Csubscriber\ze[A-Z]/customer/g
    :wqa

Three decisions are doing the work here, and none of them is a keystroke.

**The glob is the safety.** `src/domain/billing/*.ts` cannot reach the
service, the template or the migration. Compare the alternative, a
`:%s` you run in each file you happen to remember to open: the defence
there is your memory, and your memory is worse than a glob.

**`gj` builds the list without moving you.** `j` means "do not jump to the
first match" and `g` means "every match on a line, not just the first".
Leaving off `g` is the classic way to miss the second `subscriberId` on a
line that has two, and this file has one of those.

**`\ze` describes the identifiers, not the word.** `subscriber\ze[A-Z]`
matches the point just before a capital letter, so the match ends there
and the replacement leaves `Id` and `Email` alone. `subscriberId` becomes
`customerId`; the comment's bare `subscriber` is never a match at all.
`\C` makes it case sensitive regardless of the player's `ignorecase`,
which matters the day somebody writes `Subscriber` in this directory.

The instinct to fight is "rename first, check after". Renames are cheap to
run and expensive to audit. The two minutes in `:copen` reading the hits
is not overhead — it is the part of the job where you find out that one
of the fourteen is a comment and one of the other files is an API contract.

Afterwards, prove it: `:vimgrep /subscriber/gj src/**/*.ts` `:copen`
again. Three hits should remain, in two files, and you should be able to
say why each one is still there.

@start src/domain/billing/billing.entity.ts
export type Invoice = {
  id: string;
  subscriberId: string;
  subscriberEmail: string;
  total: number;
  paid: boolean;
};

@expected src/domain/billing/billing.entity.ts
export type Invoice = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  customerEmail: string;
  total: number;
  paid: boolean;
};

@start src/domain/billing/billing.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { BillingService } from '../../infrastructure/services/billing.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class BillingProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(BillingService) protected billingService: BillingService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  // subscriber numbers are issued by the billing gateway, never by us
  public async findInvoices(args: { subscriberId: string }) {
    return this.billingService.listInvoices(args.subscriberId);
  }

  public async resend(args: { subscriberId: string; subscriberEmail: string }) {
    const invoices = await this.findInvoices({ subscriberId: args.subscriberId });

    if (!invoices.length) {
      throw exception('INVOICE_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return this.emailService.send({ to: args.subscriberEmail, template: 'invoice' });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/billing/billing.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { BillingService } from '../../infrastructure/services/billing.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class BillingProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(BillingService) protected billingService: BillingService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  // subscriber numbers are issued by the billing gateway, never by us
  public async findInvoices(args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.billingService.listInvoices(args.customerId);
  }

  public async resend(args: { customerId: string; customerEmail: string }) {
    const invoices = await this.findInvoices({ customerId: args.customerId });

    if (!invoices.length) {
      throw exception('INVOICE_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return this.emailService.send({ to: args.customerEmail, template: 'invoice' });
  }
}

@start src/domain/billing/billing.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { BillingProvider } from './billing.provider';

@injectable()
export class BillingResolver {
  constructor(@inject(BillingProvider) protected billingProvider: BillingProvider) {}

  public async invoices(_: unknown, args: { subscriberId: string }) {
    return this.billingProvider.findInvoices({ subscriberId: args.subscriberId });
  }

  public async resendInvoice(_: unknown, args: { subscriberId: string }) {
    return this.billingProvider.resend(args);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/billing/billing.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { BillingProvider } from './billing.provider';

@injectable()
export class BillingResolver {
  constructor(@inject(BillingProvider) protected billingProvider: BillingProvider) {}

  public async invoices(_: unknown, args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.billingProvider.findInvoices({ customerId: args.customerId });
  }

  public async resendInvoice(_: unknown, args: { customerId: string }) {
    return this.billingProvider.resend(args);
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/billing.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class BillingService {
  public async listInvoices(customerId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/invoices/search`, {
      subscriberId: customerId,
      includeVoid: false,
    });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('BILLING_UNAVAILABLE');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/email.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class EmailService {
  public async send(args: { to: string; template: string }) {
    return this.http.post(`/send`, args);
  }

  public templates() {
    return {
      invoice: 'Hello {{subscriber}}, your invoice is ready to view.',
      reminder: 'Hello {{subscriber}}, your invoice is due on {{dueDate}}.',
    };
  }
}

@start prisma/migrations/20240211_billing/migration.sql
CREATE TABLE invoice (
  id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
  subscriber_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  subscriber_email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  total INT NOT NULL,
  paid TINYINT(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);

CREATE INDEX invoice_subscriber_id ON invoice (subscriber_id);
